course: dictionary
lesson: 11450
title: Next door and joined
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When two things adjoin, they are next to each other and joined, with no gap between them. Adjoin is a verb, so it describes an action or state: «The kitchen adjoins the dining room.» The two rooms touch along a shared wall.
explain: Look at the words around the word for clues. «Our bedroom adjoins my sister's room, so we can hear each other through the wall.» The wall is the clue: the rooms are side by side and share it, so adjoins means next to and joined.
explain: Adjoin works with rooms, buildings, fields and countries. In a map, countries that adjoin share a border. In maths, shapes that adjoin share a side. The -ing form, adjoining, is used before a noun: «adjoining rooms» means rooms that are next door and connected.
fact: Adjoin means to be next to something and joined to it.
fact: «The kitchen adjoins the dining room» means the two rooms are side by side and share a wall.
fact: Adjoining is also an adjective: «adjoining rooms» means rooms next door and connected.
fact: Countries that adjoin share a border on a map.
fact: In maths, shapes that adjoin share a side.
exercise: «The kitchen adjoins the dining room.» What does adjoins mean? | Is far away from | Is hidden behind | Is painted the same colour as | *Is next to and joined to
exercise: Look at the phrase «adjoining fields». What does adjoining mean? | *Next door and connected | Muddy and wet | Locked and closed | Empty and very big
exercise: «Spain and Portugal adjoin each other on the map.» What do the two countries share? | Their capital cities | A desert | Their flags | *A shared border
test: In «Our bedroom adjoins my sister's room», which word means next to and joined? | Bedroom | Sister's | Our | *Adjoins
test: Two shapes adjoin on a drawing. What must they share? | Nothing at all | A corner point only | *A side | Their colour
exam: Which sentence shows two fields that are next to each other and joined? | *The two fields adjoin along a shared hedge | The two fields are bright red | The two fields are sold on Friday | The two fields are very sleepy
